#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171743 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "6b20c76f-36a5-5cd2-bf15-1ce983d1d97c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5859" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{1-x}{x+2} < 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1-x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\lt 0$.\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nis $x = 1$. Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x = -2$. The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1-x$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-2)$ and $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (1,\\infty)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171744 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f18c56c1-f989-582a-bbce-ce9566d4e72d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5861" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-3} \\leq 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-3}\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x-3}\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare $x = \\pm 1$. Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$. The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-1) \\quad(-1,1) \\quad (1,3) \\quad (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$ | $\\left(-1,1\\right)$ | $\\left(1,3\\right)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$ and $(1,3)$.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $[MATH: x \\in (-\\infty,-1]\\cup [1,3)]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171758 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "51b867ab-e66b-5c82-b729-2e600df813b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7749" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\geq 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nAlso, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x = -2$ and $x=1$. The root and asymptotes divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right), \\quad \\left(-2,\\dfrac 12\\right), \\quad \\left(\\dfrac 12,1\\right), \\quad \\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$[MATH: x\\in \\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right]\\cup (1,\\infty).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f77394-4b07-5155-9d84-9871f0f1716b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{3}{2})∪(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170870} {:answer/id #uuid "c62afe1e-4d81-523e-825e-06ffab083399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-2)∪(0, \\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170871} {:answer/id #uuid "146c3d11-1efe-5d5c-b7df-ab11660c8de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170872} {:answer/id #uuid "2de572ae-6517-5bc8-aa60-fc1bd7f7f718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170873} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9d8d81-27b4-5c2b-87ea-2a0a73e0d320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 0)∪(\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d9d8d81-27b4-5c2b-87ea-2a0a73e0d320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 0)∪(\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170874} :answer-field/id #uuid "82926cb1-40a1-586c-bd66-b3a677e3f98e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170869}] :question/id #uuid "59bb1ad4-d174-5fc9-b5c8-63da66cdfefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-38509" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2x^{2} + x - 6}{x} > 0$."}]}
